e that same backgrounded shape.\012Only a drive call's return reports the green PR: `no-mistakes axi status` shows progress but never reports `checks-passed` while the ci step is still monitoring the PR for merge, so never wait on a status poll for the next gate or outcome.\012Whenever a drive call returns without a gate or an outcome - its own wait elapsed, or it was killed or timed out - reattach at once by re-running `no-mistakes axi run` without flags, backgrounded the same way; once checks are green it returns `checks-passed` immediately, and if it refuses because no run is active, read the finished outcome from `no-mistakes axi status`.\012A drive call's own wait ending, or your harness's command bound passing, never kills the run, proves failure, or permits a final timeout blocker: the measured-command instructions above own long-command limits and progress checks, so judge continued work by output growth and cumulative process-tree CPU time.\012The helper never stops a wrapped drive call for silence, because the client waits quietly on the shared daemon; only the 6 hour wall clock limit stops one. Its measured p99 x 3 for the wrapped call's own key is only a slower_than_usual report line, not a stop and not a guessed tool wait.\012The per-step durations the helper imports from completed pipeline runs are record-only under `no-mistakes-step.<step>` and never set a drive call's ceiling.\012A killed or timed-out call is never evidence the daemon died: the daemon accepts your response immediately and runs the round in the background, so the call was only ever waiting for a read while the run kept working.\012Reattach and keep going rather than reporting the pipeline blocked; rule 7 owns the checks that decide when a pipeline block is real.\012\012Two firstmate-specific rules layer on top of that guidance:\012- ask-user findings are never yours to answer: escalate to firstmate using rule 6's ask-user format and stop.\012  Firstmate applies `ask-user-authority` an